/* ==============================================================
   04 — Work zone and collision prevention
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   SetWorkZoneLimits, SetToolSphere, SetCollisionCfg
   NEEDS     Read this one before running anything. Most of these
             commands require the robot to be DEACTIVATED.
   WATCH     The robot refusing a move it could physically make
   TIME      setup, then ~20 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   READ THIS FIRST

   This file is mostly documentation. The important commands here
   CANNOT go in a motion program, because they can only be
   executed while the robot is deactivated:

       SetWorkZoneLimits(...)     deactivated only
       SetWorkZoneCfg(...)        deactivated only
       SetToolSphere(...)         deactivated only
       SetCollisionCfg(...)       deactivated only

   That is deliberate. These are safety boundaries, not process
   parameters — a running program must not be able to widen its
   own cage. Set them once, from the MecaPortal, with the robot
   deactivated. They are persistent and survive a power cycle.

   The runnable part of this file is at the bottom.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   1. THE WORK ZONE

       SetWorkZoneLimits(xmin, ymin, zmin, xmax, ymax, zmax)

   A cuboid, in millimetres, defined in the BRF — the robot's own
   base frame, NOT the WRF. This catches people out: the work
   zone is a property of the cell, so it deliberately does not
   move when you redefine the world frame.

   Inside the box, the robot works. Try to leave it, and the
   robot stops the move before it happens.

   The factory default is plus or minus 10,000 mm in every
   direction — effectively off.

       SetWorkZoneCfg(l, m)

     l — what happens on a breach
         0  silent, logged internally only
         1  warning [2183]
         4  warning [2183] and a motion error [3049]   (default)

     m — what gets checked
         1  the end of the shaft only                  (default)
         2  the tool as well (the tool sphere, below)
         3  the tool AND every robot link

   On a SCARA, the zone you will reach for first is a FLOOR. The
   arm itself sweeps a flat disc well above the bench; the part
   that goes down into the work is the shaft and whatever hangs
   off it. A zmin just above the fixture is the cheapest
   protection there is against a typo in a z coordinate.

   Mode 3 is the one to talk about with an integrator. It checks
   the ELBOW and the outer link, not just the tool — and on a
   SCARA those sweep wide, out to the side of the line from base
   to tool. Lefty and righty put the elbow on OPPOSITE sides, so
   one posture can keep the whole arm inside a zone that the
   other posture breaks, for the same tool position. Anyone who
   has watched a robot put its elbow through a light curtain
   understands immediately why this matters.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   2. THE TOOL SPHERE

       SetToolSphere(x, y, z, r)

   The robot knows its own geometry. It does not know about your
   tool, so you describe it as a single sphere: a centre in the
   FRF — the end of the shaft, z up — and a radius, in mm.

   Crude on purpose. A sphere is cheap to check thousands of
   times a second, and for a compact tool it is a reasonable
   envelope. Once defined, the sphere is included in work zone
   checks (modes 2 and 3) and in self-collision checks.

   Two things to know on this robot. The tool hangs below the
   shaft, so the sphere's centre has a NEGATIVE z — the same sign
   rule as the TRF. And the manual is specific about one thing
   that is NOT checked: the tool sphere against the spline shaft
   itself. Size the sphere to the real tool.

   Default is all zeros, which disables it.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   3. SELF-COLLISION

       SetCollisionCfg(l)

     l   0  silent, logged internally
         1  warning [2182] on each new imminent collision
         4  warning [2182] and a motion error [3041]   (default)

   The robot continuously checks whether it is about to run into
   itself, including the tool sphere if you defined one. On a
   SCARA that mostly means one thing: the elbow folding the outer
   link, and the tool, back towards the base column. It is on by
   default at the strictest setting, which is the right default —
   leave it there.

   Note the word IMMINENT. These are all PREVENTION features, not
   detection: the robot refuses the motion before the collision,
   rather than noticing afterwards. That distinction is worth
   making explicitly, because "collision detection" on many
   robots means "we stop shortly after the crash".

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   THE SALES POINT

   An MCS500 usually sits on a bench, inside a machine, next to
   expensive fixtures, often within reach of a person. The work
   zone is what lets an integrator promise that the robot cannot
   reach the loading window while an operator's hand is in it —
   enforced by the controller, checked before every move,
   persistent across reboots, and not modifiable by the running
   program.

   Be careful with the words, though. This is a motion boundary,
   not a certified safety function. It prevents mistakes and
   protects equipment. For personnel safety, refer to the safety
   chapter of the user manual and to the robot's actual safety
   signals — the E-Stop, P-Stop 1 and P-Stop 2 inputs on its
   power supply. Do not let a work zone be sold as a safety
   rating it does not have.

   ------------------------------------------------------------
   SETUP: RUN THESE WITH THE ROBOT DEACTIVATED

   Copy these into the code editor, deactivate the robot, run
   them, then reactivate before continuing.

       SetToolSphere(0, 0, -15, 15)
       SetCollisionCfg(4)
       SetWorkZoneCfg(4, 2)
       SetWorkZoneLimits(-300, -300, 40, 300, 300, 400)

   That declares a 15 mm tool sphere centred 15 mm below the end
   of the shaft — an envelope for the house tool of
   intermediate/02, 30 mm long — full error reporting on
   collisions, work zone checking of the tool sphere (mode 2),
   and a floor at z = 40 mm in the base frame. The other
   five faces are out of the arm's reach, so in practice this
   zone is "nothing goes below 40 mm".

   To undo it all afterwards, deactivate and run:

       SetWorkZoneLimits(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
       SetWorkZoneCfg(4, 1)
       SetToolSphere(0, 0, 0, 0)
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============ RUNNABLE PART ================================= */
/* Requires the SETUP block above to have been applied. With it
   applied, nothing — shaft end or tool sphere — may go below
   z = 40 mm. With no tool fitted the sphere is imaginary, which
   is the point: the robot protects the tool you DECLARED. */

/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(80)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)

// Well inside the zone: the bottom of the tool sphere is 70 mm
// up. Runs normally.
MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
Delay(1)

// Still inside, near the floor: the sphere bottom is at 45 mm.
// Runs normally.
MovePose(170, 0, 75, 0)
Delay(1)

// The end of the shaft would stop at 60 mm, inside the zone —
// but the tool sphere would reach down to 30 mm, through the
// floor. The move starts, and the robot stops it at the
// boundary with a work zone error [3049].
MovePose(170, 0, 60, 0)
Delay(1)

// If you reset and resume, execution carries on from here.
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()

/* Out of the work zone. The end of the shaft itself would be at
   20 mm, below the floor. The robot REFUSES this move and
   reports a work zone error [3049]. It does not move part of the
   way and stop — it does not start.

   This is the line to show a customer. The robot is entirely
   capable of reaching that position, and declines to.

   After it errors, reset the error and resume to continue: press
   the run button (now red), then the resume button. */
MovePose(170, 0, 20, 0)

// If you reset and resume, execution carries on from here.
Delay(1)
MovePose(170, 0, 100, 0)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run to the refused move and let it error. Then say: the
      robot can reach there, and it will not. Show the error code
      in the log panel.

   2. Set SetWorkZoneCfg(4, 1) — shaft end only — and run the
      60 mm move again. It is allowed: the shaft stays above the
      floor and nobody is checking the tool. Switch back to mode 2
      and it is not. This makes the modes concrete in about a
      minute.

   3. For the elbow argument, put a wall in the zone instead of a
      floor — say ymin = -100, so the robot must stay out of the
      area to its right — and try mode 3. Reach a point just
      inside the wall in each posture, with SetConf(1) and
      SetConf(-1). Whether the elbow stays inside depends on the
      posture, and the robot will tell you which one breaks it.

   NEXT   05_payload_and_torque_limits.mxprog
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
